{"notes":[{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/ondrejbohdal/meta-calibration"},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/6ccf07be9d623bc1bf0de0a865400ecdcb1ad28f.zip"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration of neural networks is a topical problem that is becoming more and more important as neural networks increasingly underpin real-world applications. The problem is especially noticeable when using modern neural networks, for which there is a significant difference between the confidence of the model and the probability of correct prediction. Various strategies have been proposed to improve calibration, yet accurate calibration remains challenging. We propose a novel framework with two contributions: introducing a new differentiable surrogate for expected calibration error (DECE) that allows calibration quality to be directly optimised, and a meta-learning framework that uses DECE to optimise for validation set calibration with respect to model hyper-parameters. The results show that we achieve competitive performance with existing calibration approaches. Our framework opens up a new avenue and toolset for tackling calibration, which we believe will inspire further work on this important challenge."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nbohdal2023metacalibration,\ntitle={Meta-Calibration: Learning of Model Calibration Using Differentiable Expected Calibration Error},\nauthor={Ondrej Bohdal and Yongxin Yang and Timothy Hospedales},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=R2hUure38l},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Meta-Calibration: Learning of Model Calibration Using Differentiable Expected Calibration Error"},"license":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f9266913213b59a701bf7cab86d908318fa544ab.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"bohdal|metacalibration_learning_of_model_calibration_using_differentiable_expected_calibration_error"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ondrej_Bohdal1","~Yongxin_Yang1","~Timothy_Hospedales1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Yingzhen_Li1"},"authors":{"value":["Ondrej Bohdal","Yongxin Yang","Timothy Hospedales"]}},"tmdate":1726599551414,"pdate":1692835634087,"tcdate":1684868353601,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper1189/Authors"],"forum":"R2hUure38l","number":1189,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1684868353601,"mdate":1726599551414,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper1189/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/Paper1189/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"odate":1685036560103,"domain":"TMLR","id":"R2hUure38l","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/kaspar98/lece-calibration"},"abstract":{"value":"A probabilistic classifier is considered calibrated if it outputs probabilities equal to the expected class distribution given the classifier's output. Calibration is essential in safety-critical tasks where small deviations between the predicted probabilities and the actually observed class proportions can incur high costs. A common approach to improve the calibration of a classifier is to use a hold-out data set and a post-hoc calibration method to learn a correcting transformation for the classifier's output. This work explores the field of post-hoc calibration methods for multi-class classifiers and formulates two assumptions about the probability simplex which have been used by many existing non-parametric calibration methods, but despite this, have never been explicitly stated: assuming locally equal label distributions or assuming locally equal calibration errors. Based on the latter assumption, an intuitive non-parametric post-hoc calibration method is proposed, which is shown to offer improvements to the state-of-the-art according to the expected calibration error metric on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 data sets.\n"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nvalk2023assuming,\ntitle={Assuming Locally Equal Calibration Errors for Non-Parametric Multiclass Calibration},\nauthor={Kaspar Valk and Meelis Kull},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=na5sHG69rI},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Assuming Locally Equal Calibration Errors for Non-Parametric Multiclass Calibration"},"certifications":{"value":[]},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"We have moved to the appendix:\n* the toy example of different calibration definitions from Section 2.2,\n* the discussion on computational and memory complexity of LECE calibration from Section 3.2,\n* the discussion on running times of LECE calibration from Section 4.2.\n\nWe have also removed the colors which denoted the changes done addressing the reviewers' feedback.\n\nWe have done minor edits to improve the layout and locations of the tables and figures in the paper.\n\nWe have deanonymized the paper, added an acknowledgements section, and added an URL to the source code of the experiments to the end of the introduction."},"license":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/573d7225ebae5264b539d08f75924b862467a10e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"valk|assuming_locally_equal_calibration_errors_for_nonparametric_multiclass_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Kaspar_Valk1","~Meelis_Kull1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Aditya_Menon1"},"authors":{"value":["Kaspar Valk","Meelis Kull"]}},"tmdate":1726599411096,"pdate":1686025803629,"tcdate":1676907668657,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper879/Authors"],"forum":"na5sHG69rI","number":879,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1676907668657,"mdate":1726599411096,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/Paper879/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper879/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"odate":1676987393704,"domain":"TMLR","id":"na5sHG69rI","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Model miscalibration has been frequently identified in modern deep neural networks. Recent work aims to improve model calibration directly through a differentiable calibration proxy. However, the calibration produced is often biased due to the binning mechanism. In this work, we propose to learn better-calibrated models via meta-regularization, which has two components: (1) gamma network (gamma-net), a meta learner that outputs sample-wise gamma value (continuous variable) for Focal loss for regularizing the backbone network; (2) smooth expected calibration error (SECE), a Gaussian-kernel based, unbiased, and differentiable surrogate to ECE that enables the smooth optimization of gamma-net. We evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed approach in regularizing neural networks towards improved and unbiased calibration on three computer vision datasets. We empirically demonstrate that: (a) learning sample-wise $\\gamma$ as continuous variables can effectively improve calibration; (b) SECE smoothly optimizes gamma-net towards unbiased and robust calibration with respect to the binning schemes; and (c) the combination of gamma-net and SECE achieves the best calibration performance across various calibration metrics while retaining very competitive predictive performance as compared to multiple recently proposed methods."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nwang2024towards,\ntitle={Towards Unbiased Calibration using Meta-Regularization},\nauthor={Cheng Wang and Jacek Golebiowski},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Yf8iHCfG4W},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Towards Unbiased Calibration using Meta-Regularization"},"certifications":{"value":["J2C Certification"]},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"We have incorporated the action editor's comment and implemented the following revisions into the camera ready version:\n\n1. We addressed the final comment from the action editor by stressing the significance of our proposed method in the Section 5.1.\n\n2. Restructured the Section 3 Preliminaries in a more understandable way\n\n3. Fixed formatting/typos."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/70916c60aa137204e2d75f41503d9236b497e8b0.pdf"},"event_certifications":{"value":["iclr.cc/ICLR/2025/Journal_Track"]},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|towards_unbiased_calibration_using_metaregularization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Cheng_Wang9","~Jacek_Golebiowski1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Bruno_Loureiro1"},"authors":{"value":["Cheng Wang","Jacek Golebiowski"]}},"tmdate":1761078627818,"pdate":1718897348201,"tcdate":1709994931045,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper2358/Authors"],"forum":"Yf8iHCfG4W","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2358,"cdate":1709994931045,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper2358/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/Paper2358/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted","TMLR/-/Event_Certification"],"mdate":1761078627818,"odate":1711325175207,"domain":"TMLR","id":"Yf8iHCfG4W","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/yungchentang/NCToolkit"},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/6d8910d60d8dccbf0ba09560d06b72971eb46569.pdf"},"abstract":{"value":"Neural network calibration is an essential task in deep learning to ensure consistency between the confidence of model prediction and the true correctness likelihood. In this paper, we propose a new post-processing calibration method called $\\textbf{Neural Clamping}$, which employs a simple joint input-output transformation on a pre-trained classifier via a learnable universal input perturbation and an output temperature scaling parameter. Moreover, we provide theoretical explanations on why Neural Clamping is provably better than temperature scaling. Evaluated on BloodMNIST, CIFAR-100, and ImageNet image recognition datasets and a variety of deep neural network models, our empirical results show that Neural Clamping significantly outperforms state-of-the-art post-processing calibration methods. The code is available at github.com/yungchentang/NCToolkit, and the demo is available at huggingface.co/spaces/TrustSafeAI/NCTV."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\ntang2024neural,\ntitle={Neural Clamping: Joint Input Perturbation and Temperature Scaling for Neural Network Calibration},\nauthor={Yung-Chen Tang and Pin-Yu Chen and Tsung-Yi Ho},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=qSFToMqLcq},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Neural Clamping: Joint Input Perturbation and Temperature Scaling for Neural Network Calibration"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"We have made minor changes based on the reviewers' suggestions. We added the code link and demo link in the abstract. According to Reviewer pRL2's suggestion, we highlighted in Section 4.3 that theoretically derived input perturbation can achieve performance similar to that of training results. Based on Reviewer 7Ruf's suggestion, we explained the reasons for such an experimental setup in Appendix G."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/959eb91fd2e6f7851ab8e28bc9b9ba43dfd350f8.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"tang|neural_clamping_joint_input_perturbation_and_temperature_scaling_for_neural_network_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yung-Chen_Tang1","~Pin-Yu_Chen1","~Tsung-Yi_Ho2"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Bruno_Loureiro1"},"authors":{"value":["Yung-Chen Tang","Pin-Yu Chen","Tsung-Yi Ho"]}},"tmdate":1726599774765,"pdate":1721550245258,"tcdate":1714489391668,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper2601/Authors"],"forum":"qSFToMqLcq","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2601,"cdate":1714489391668,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/Paper2601/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/Paper2601/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1726599774765,"odate":1714514312785,"domain":"TMLR","id":"qSFToMqLcq","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/neurodata/kdg"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep discriminative approaches, such as decision forests and deep neural networks, have recently found applications in many important real-world scenarios. However, deploying these learning algorithms in safety-critical applications raises concerns, particularly when it comes to ensuring calibration for both in-distribution and out-of-distribution regions. Many popular methods for in-distribution (ID) calibration, such as isotonic and Platt’s sigmoidal regression, exhibit adequate ID calibration performance. However, these methods are not calibrated for the entire feature space, leading to overconfidence in the out-of-distribution (OOD) region. Existing OOD calibration methods generally exhibit poor ID calibration. In this paper, we jointly address the ID and OOD problems. We leveraged the fact that deep models learn to partition feature space into a union of polytopes, that is, flat-sided geometric objects. We introduce a geodesic distance to measure the distance between these polytopes and further distinguish samples within the same polytope using a Gaussian kernel. Our experiments on both tabular and vision benchmarks show that the proposed approaches, namely Kernel Density Forest (KDF) and Kernel Density Network (KDN), obtain well-calibrated posteriors for both ID and OOD samples,  while mostly preserving the classification accuracy and extrapolating beyond the training data to handle OOD inputs appropriately."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\ndey2025simple,\ntitle={Simple Calibration via Geodesic Kernels},\nauthor={Jayanta Dey and Haoyin Xu and Ashwin De Silva and Joshua T Vogelstein},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=dpcRp8ix5T},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Simple Calibration via Geodesic Kernels"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"We sincerely thank TMLR and the action editor for their help and guidance throughout the reviews. In the following we describe how we have improved our paper by incorporating the minor revisions as suggested by the action editor.\n\n> “With overparameterized networks, which is the norm, they typically exhibit this property that the number of populated polytopes is equal to the sample size.” Reviewer CZ96 suggested that this point should be clearly stated in the paper.\n\nWe have added the above line to the FAQ section (Page 17) in response to the question: \"Number of polytopes grows exponentially with the size of network. Isn’t the approach computationally infeasible?\" as suggested by Reviewer CZ96.\n\n>  It would strengthen the paper to include a discussion on how the proposed method could generalize to more complex datasets and deeper neural networks.\n\nWe have added the following lines to the discussion:\n``The vision experiments in this paper show how the model can be applied to deeper networks by employing a front-end encoder to extract local image features (as explained in the first paragraph of Section 3.2). Instead of applying KDN to the entire ViT, we applied it solely to the final fully connected layers with ReLU activations. This reduces the effective depth of the model, ensuring that KDN can be applied even to encoders that do not rely on ReLU activations.\"\n\n``However, a key limitation of our approach is the need to store parameters for each populated polytope, which may hinder scalability to very large datasets. One potential solution is to merge nearby polytopes into a single representative polytope.\""},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e16dcdca3b01d0c287c839821021e9e70dd3cb6d.pdf"},"event_certifications":{"value":["lifelong-ml.cc/CoLLAs/2025/Journal_Track"]},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"dey|simple_calibration_via_geodesic_kernels"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jayanta_Dey1","~Haoyin_Xu1","~Ashwin_De_Silva1","~Joshua_T_Vogelstein1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Weijian_Deng1"},"authors":{"value":["Jayanta Dey","Haoyin Xu","Ashwin De Silva","Joshua T Vogelstein"]}},"tmdate":1766063223623,"pdate":1749705748841,"tcdate":1740190682651,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper4311/Authors"],"forum":"dpcRp8ix5T","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4311,"cdate":1740190682651,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper4311/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper4311/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted","TMLR/-/Event_Certification"],"mdate":1766063223623,"odate":1740587779759,"domain":"TMLR","id":"dpcRp8ix5T","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Under review for TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Sparse and low-rank adaptation impose different structures on a weight update. Combining them under a fixed parameter budget raises a common question: how should allocation and calibration signals be chosen to support downstream learning while preserving pretrained knowledge? We study these choices with Residual Sparse and Low-Rank Adaptation (ReSLo), which initializes low-rank factors from a dense task gradient and selects sparse blocks from its residual. A local analysis relates the energy recovered by sparse blocks to the reconstruction cost of reducing rank. Experiments across knowledge, mathematical reasoning, and code generation show that local reconstruction and downstream performance can favor different allocations. Paired evaluation further shows that similar aggregate knowledge accuracy can conceal different preservation of the pretrained model's correct answers. Larger calibration sets improve gradient agreement and support stability. Selecting blocks from the gradient residual captures more independent reference-gradient energy outside the initial low-rank tangent space than random selection. These findings connect allocation choices to functional objectives and distinguish signal quality at initialization from performance after training. They provide guidance for comparing sparse and low-rank updates through task performance, explicit retention measures, and calibration diagnostics."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nanonymous2026sparse,\ntitle={Sparse and Low-Rank Adaptation: Allocation, Calibration, and Knowledge Retention},\nauthor={Anonymous},\njournal={Submitted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=CxJ0FAeSc7},\nnote={Under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Sparse and Low-Rank Adaptation: Allocation, Calibration, and Knowledge Retention"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ba9a066167538aef4077c0e91e608d2dbed53143.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR/Under_Review"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Lei_Wang13"}},"tmdate":1793853730490,"tcdate":1792327438101,"writers":["TMLR","TMLR/Paper13463/Authors"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper13463/Authors"],"forum":"CxJ0FAeSc7","number":13463,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1792327438101,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/Paper13463/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review"],"mdate":1793853730490,"odate":1793853728852,"domain":"TMLR","id":"CxJ0FAeSc7","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/woodyx218/opacus_global_clipping"},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/71ad5dc905cd1c4db109e158709482e6d2ddaaba.zip"},"abstract":{"value":"Differentially private (DP) training preserves the data privacy usually at the cost of slower convergence (and thus lower accuracy), as well as more severe mis-calibration than its non-private counterpart. To analyze the convergence of DP training, we formulate a continuous time analysis through the lens of neural tangent kernel (NTK), which characterizes the per-sample gradient clipping and the noise addition in DP training, for arbitrary network architectures and loss functions. Interestingly, we show that the noise addition only affects the privacy risk but not the convergence or calibration, whereas the per-sample gradient clipping (under both flat and layerwise clipping styles) only affects the convergence and calibration.\n\nFurthermore, we observe that while DP models trained with small clipping norm usually achieve the best accurate, but are poorly calibrated and thus unreliable. In sharp contrast, DP models trained with large clipping norm enjoy the same privacy guarantee and similar accuracy, but are significantly more \\textit{calibrated}. Our code can be found at https://github.com/woodyx218/opacus_global_clipping."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nbu2023on,\ntitle={On the Convergence and Calibration of Deep Learning with Differential Privacy},\nauthor={Zhiqi Bu and Hua Wang and Zongyu Dai and Qi Long},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=K0CAGgjYS1},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"On the Convergence and Calibration of Deep Learning with Differential Privacy"},"certifications":{"value":[]},"license":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e6ebc1129469f205507df3d3cd60a41feba1394f.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"bu|on_the_convergence_and_calibration_of_deep_learning_with_differential_privacy"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhiqi_Bu1","~Hua_Wang7","~Zongyu_Dai1","~Qi_Long1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Sebastian_U_Stich1"},"authors":{"value":["Zhiqi Bu","Hua Wang","Zongyu Dai","Qi Long"]}},"tmdate":1726599599105,"pdate":1686554035034,"tcdate":1678759114198,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper943/Authors"],"forum":"K0CAGgjYS1","number":943,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1678759114198,"mdate":1726599599105,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/Paper943/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper943/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"odate":1679046023699,"domain":"TMLR","id":"K0CAGgjYS1","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Machine learning–based segmentation models are frequently miscalibrated, producing for each pixel a label prediction whose associated confidence does not accurately reflect the true underlying probability.  This limitation is particularly critical in high-stakes applications such as medical image segmentation. Although several calibration methods have been proposed, only a small subset of them provides theoretical guarantees on the calibration error, i.e., they do not typically ensure that, with high probability, the model exhibits a low calibration error. In this work, we develop a post-hoc calibration method for which formal calibration guarantees can be established. To achieve this, we derive from standard concentration inequalities an improved theoretical bound that explicitly accounts for the spatial dependence inherent in binary segmentation outputs. We empirically demonstrate that the proposed calibrator satisfies the derived guarantee and outperforms existing post-hoc calibration approaches."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nviti2026finitesample,\ntitle={Finite-sample Calibration Guarantees for Binary Segmentation},\nauthor={Bruno Viti and Martin Holler},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=fHHbzZZ2Na},\nnote={J2C Certification}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Finite-sample Calibration Guarantees for Binary Segmentation"},"certifications":{"value":["J2C Certification"]},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/0b3e91d8087229ee21bb90d7a6fecf004441a5ce.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"viti|finitesample_calibration_guarantees_for_binary_segmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Bruno_Viti1","~Martin_Holler1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Nhat_Ho1"},"authors":{"value":["Bruno Viti","Martin Holler"]}},"tmdate":1793320374789,"pdate":1793320374671,"tcdate":1782121993229,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper9939/Authors"],"forum":"fHHbzZZ2Na","number":9939,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1782121993229,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/Paper9939/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper9939/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1793320374789,"odate":1782206965409,"domain":"TMLR","id":"fHHbzZZ2Na","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/SebGGruber/Optimizing_Calibration_Estimators"},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/7250fb20f4d12e1cdde2a6b35dd3dcbb208e997f.zip"},"abstract":{"value":"In this work, we propose a mean-squared error-based risk that enables the comparison and optimization of estimators of squared calibration errors in practical settings.\nImproving the calibration of classifiers is crucial for enhancing the trustworthiness and interpretability of machine learning models, especially in sensitive decision-making scenarios.\nAlthough various calibration (error) estimators exist in the current literature, there is a lack of guidance on selecting the appropriate estimator and tuning its hyperparameters.\nBy leveraging the bilinear structure of squared calibration errors, we reformulate calibration estimation as a regression problem with independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) input pairs.\nThis reformulation allows us to quantify the performance of different estimators even for the most challenging calibration criterion, known as canonical calibration.\nOur approach advocates for a training-validation-testing pipeline when estimating a calibration error on an evaluation dataset.\nWe demonstrate the effectiveness of our pipeline by optimizing existing calibration estimators and comparing them with novel kernel ridge regression-based estimators on standard image classification tasks."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\ngruber2025optimizing,\ntitle={Optimizing Estimators of Squared Calibration Errors in Classification},\nauthor={Sebastian Gregor Gruber and Francis R. Bach},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=BPDVZajOW5},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Optimizing Estimators of Squared Calibration Errors in Classification"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/401626c0d371373e6ec74d996640e07e39771659.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"gruber|optimizing_estimators_of_squared_calibration_errors_in_classification"},"authorids":{"value":["~Sebastian_Gregor_Gruber1","~Francis_R._Bach1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Masha_Itkina1"},"authors":{"value":["Sebastian Gregor Gruber","Francis R. Bach"]}},"tmdate":1741051697819,"pdate":1741051697751,"tcdate":1728488878572,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper3468/Authors"],"forum":"BPDVZajOW5","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":3468,"cdate":1728488878572,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper3468/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper3468/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1741051697819,"odate":1730475003097,"domain":"TMLR","id":"BPDVZajOW5","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/beijifeng02/Does_Confidence_Calibration_Improve_Conformal_Prediction"},"abstract":{"value":"Conformal prediction is an emerging technique for uncertainty quantification that constructs prediction sets guaranteed to contain the true label with a predefined probability. Previous works often employ temperature scaling to calibrate classifiers, assuming that confidence calibration benefits conformal prediction. However, the specific impact of confidence calibration on conformal prediction remains underexplored. In this work, we make two key discoveries about the impact of confidence calibration methods on adaptive conformal prediction. Firstly, we empirically show that current confidence calibration methods (e.g., temperature scaling) typically lead to larger prediction sets in adaptive conformal prediction. Secondly, by investigating the role of temperature value, we observe that high-confidence predictions can enhance the efficiency of adaptive conformal prediction. Theoretically, we prove that predictions with higher confidence result in smaller prediction sets on expectation. This finding implies that the rescaling parameters in these calibration methods, when optimized with cross-entropy loss, might counteract the goal of generating efficient prediction sets. To address this issue, we propose \\textbf{Conformal Temperature Scaling} (ConfTS), a variant of temperature scaling with a novel loss function designed to enhance the efficiency of prediction sets. This approach can be extended to optimize the parameters of other post-hoc methods of confidence calibration. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method improves existing adaptive conformal prediction methods in both image and text classification tasks."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nxi2025does,\ntitle={Does confidence calibration improve conformal prediction?},\nauthor={Huajun Xi and Jianguo Huang and Kangdao Liu and Lei Feng and Hongxin Wei},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=6DDaTwTvdE},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Does confidence calibration improve conformal prediction?"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/39f4d6d7b261f66828dd9accdcc3bb54793257b0.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"xi|does_confidence_calibration_improve_conformal_prediction"},"authorids":{"value":["~Huajun_Xi2","~Jianguo_Huang2","~Kangdao_Liu1","~Lei_Feng1","~Hongxin_Wei1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Matthew_J._Holland1"},"authors":{"value":["Huajun Xi","Jianguo Huang","Kangdao Liu","Lei Feng","Hongxin Wei"]}},"tmdate":1785745239882,"pdate":1751846076949,"tcdate":1741537150577,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper4430/Authors"],"forum":"6DDaTwTvdE","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4430,"cdate":1741537150577,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper4430/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper4430/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1785745239882,"odate":1743155194446,"domain":"TMLR","id":"6DDaTwTvdE","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/wkiri/simcalib"},"abstract":{"value":"Trustworthy classifiers are essential to the adoption of machine learning predictions in many real-world settings. The predicted probability of possible outcomes can inform high-stakes decision making, particularly when assessing the expected value of alternative decisions or the risk of bad outcomes. These decisions require well-calibrated probabilities, not just the correct prediction of the most likely class. Black-box classifier calibration methods can improve the reliability of a classifier’s output without requiring retraining. However, these methods are unable to detect subpopulations where calibration could also improve prediction accuracy. Such subpopulations are said to exhibit “hidden heterogeneity” (HH), because the original classifier did not detect them. The paper proposes a quantitative measure for HH. It also introduces two similarity-weighted calibration methods that can address HH by adapting locally to each test item: SWC weights the calibration set by similarity to the test item, and SWC-HH explicitly incorporates hidden heterogeneity to filter the calibration set. Experiments show that the improvements in calibration achieved by similarity-based calibration methods correlate with the amount of HH present and, given sufficient calibration data, generally exceed calibration achieved by global methods. HH can therefore serve as a useful diagnostic tool for identifying when local calibration methods would be beneficial."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nwagstaff2023hidden,\ntitle={Hidden Heterogeneity: When to Choose Similarity-Based Calibration},\nauthor={Kiri L. Wagstaff and Thomas G Dietterich},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=RA0TDqt3hC},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Hidden Heterogeneity: When to Choose Similarity-Based Calibration"},"certifications":{"value":[]},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"We accepted the changes previously highlighted in blue.  There are no further changes. \n"},"license":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/15eb3c737510c7b6dcc23d3ef1f1ed529b48f00f.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"wagstaff|hidden_heterogeneity_when_to_choose_similaritybased_calibration","readers":["everyone"]},"authorids":{"readers":["everyone"],"value":["~Kiri_L._Wagstaff1","~Thomas_G_Dietterich1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Aditya_Menon1"},"authors":{"readers":["everyone"],"value":["Kiri L. Wagstaff","Thomas G Dietterich"]}},"tmdate":1726599550131,"pdate":1673295447438,"tcdate":1665112319415,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper486/Authors"],"forum":"RA0TDqt3hC","number":486,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1665112319415,"mdate":1726599550131,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper486/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper486/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted","TMLR/-/Edit"],"odate":1665160055322,"domain":"TMLR","id":"RA0TDqt3hC","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/fiducio-ai/Fiducio"},"abstract":{"value":"Reliable confidence estimates are essential in semantic segmentation, especially in safetycritical settings where overconfident errors can mislead downstream decisions. Yet modern segmentation models often remain miscalibrated. Post-hoc calibration offers a practical way to correct confidence estimates without retraining the segmentation model, but its use in dense prediction raises structural issues that are often overlooked. We study two such issues. First, adding a constant to all logits leaves the softmax probabilities unchanged, but several standard calibrators can still depend on this arbitrary offset. As a result, two logit representations encoding the same predictive distribution may yield different calibrated probabilities. This non-identifiability also exists in image classification. In segmentation, however, the offset can vary across pixels or voxels, creating spatially varying representation dependence. We define translation-invariant (TI) calibrators as those whose outputs are unchanged under such shifts, characterize which common calibrators satisfy this property, and construct TI counterparts of shift-sensitive calibrators to isolate the effect of removing representation dependence. Second, post-hoc calibration is typically fitted by minimizing a likelihood-based objective, whereas segmentation models are trained with objectives that combine cross-entropy with task-specific terms such as soft Dice. Fitting a calibrator on a held-out set using cross-entropy loss can degrade the quality of the segmentation map, due to the mismatch between the training and calibration losses and the small size of the calibration set. We study decision-preserving calibration under argmax- and order-preservation constraints. Since enforcing these constraints collapses affine softmax calibrators to temperature scaling, we introduce class-conditional affine calibrators that can be made argmax- or order-preserving while retaining greater expressivity, allowing us to quantify the calibration–segmentation trade-off induced by decision preservation. Across natural-image and medical segmentation benchmarks, and under corruption-based covariate shift, matched comparisons show that TI variants generally improve calibration metrics, while decision-preserving variants prevent segmentation degradation by construction and retain strong calibration performance. These results provide practical design principles for well-defined post-hoc calibration pipelines in semantic segmentation."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nkirscher2026rethinking,\ntitle={Rethinking Post-Hoc Calibration in Semantic Segmentation},\nauthor={Tristan Kirscher and Kim-Celine Kahl and Maximilian Rouven Rokuss and Balint Kovacs and Klaus Maier-Hein and Xavier Coubez and MEYER Philippe and Sylvain Faisan},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=xwNoSNxgxV},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Rethinking Post-Hoc Calibration in Semantic Segmentation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3ad862d8fc945cdd14b23ea9623727bdc873ab46.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"kirscher|rethinking_posthoc_calibration_in_semantic_segmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tristan_Kirscher1","~Kim-Celine_Kahl1","~Maximilian_Rouven_Rokuss1","~Balint_Kovacs1","~Klaus_Maier-Hein1","~Xavier_Coubez1","~MEYER_Philippe1","~Sylvain_Faisan2"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Jose_Dolz1"},"authors":{"value":["Tristan Kirscher","Kim-Celine Kahl","Maximilian Rouven Rokuss","Balint Kovacs","Klaus Maier-Hein","Xavier Coubez","MEYER Philippe","Sylvain Faisan"]}},"tmdate":1791030023825,"pdate":1791030023742,"tcdate":1782972160026,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper10198/Authors"],"forum":"xwNoSNxgxV","number":10198,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1782972160026,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper10198/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper10198/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1791030023825,"odate":1783002069478,"domain":"TMLR","id":"xwNoSNxgxV","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/yangy96/multiqa-calib"},"abstract":{"value":"Multilingual pre-trained Large Language Models (LLMs) are incredibly effective at Question Answering (QA), a core task in Natural Language Understanding, achieving high accuracies on several multilingual benchmarks. However, little is known about how well their confidences are calibrated. In this paper, we comprehensively benchmark the calibration of several multilingual LLMs (MLLMs) on a variety of QA tasks. We perform extensive experiments, spanning encoder-only, encoder-decoder, and decoder-only QA models (size varying from 110M to 7B parameters) and diverse languages, including both high- and low-resource ones. We study different dimensions of calibration in in-distribution, out-of-distribution, and cross-lingual transfer settings, and investigate strategies to improve it, including post-hoc methods and regularized fine-tuning. For decoder-only LLMs such as LlaMa2, we additionally find that in-context learning improves confidence calibration on multilingual data.\nWe also conduct several ablation experiments to study the effect of language distances, language corpus size, and model size on calibration, and how multilingual models compare with their monolingual counterparts for diverse tasks and languages. Our experiments suggest that the multilingual QA models are poorly calibrated for languages other than English and incorporating a small set of cheaply translated multilingual samples during fine-tuning/calibration effectively enhances the calibration performance."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nyang2026on,\ntitle={On Calibration of Multilingual Question Answering {LLM}s},\nauthor={Yahan Yang and Soham Dan and Dan Roth and Insup Lee},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=4klghu2PTj},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"On Calibration of Multilingual Question Answering LLMs"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/4e0f06fdec311fd9d2a15ee1deff4663aa6dd183.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"yang|on_calibration_of_multilingual_question_answering_llms"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yahan_Yang1","~Soham_Dan1","~Dan_Roth3","~Insup_Lee1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Serguei_Barannikov1"},"authors":{"value":["Yahan Yang","Soham Dan","Dan Roth","Insup Lee"]}},"tmdate":1767527722834,"pdate":1767527722780,"tcdate":1752521951613,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper5379/Authors"],"forum":"4klghu2PTj","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":5379,"cdate":1752521951613,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper5379/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper5379/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1767527722834,"odate":1753461604348,"domain":"TMLR","id":"4klghu2PTj","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/fd6566d434661f75248ce2d3931506f7d2310f07.zip"},"abstract":{"value":"Data-sparse settings such as robotic manipulation, molecular physics, and galaxy morphology classification are some of the hardest domains for deep learning. For these problems, equivariant networks can help improve modeling across undersampled parts of the input space, and uncertainty estimation can guard against overconfidence. However, until now, the relationships between equivariance and model confidence, and more generally equivariance and model calibration, has yet to be studied. Since traditional classification and regression error terms show up in the definitions of calibration error, it is natural to suspect that previous work can be used to help understand the relationship between equivariance and calibration error. In this work, we present a theory relating equivariance to uncertainty estimation. By proving lower and upper bounds on uncertainty calibration errors (ECE and ENCE) under various equivariance conditions, we elucidate the generalization limits of equivariant models and illustrate how symmetry mismatch can result in miscalibration in both classification and regression. We complement our theoretical framework with numerical experiments that clarify the relationship between equivariance and uncertainty using a variety of real and simulated datasets, and we comment on trends with symmetry mismatch, group size, and aleatoric and epistemic uncertainties."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nberman2026on,\ntitle={On Uncertainty Calibration for Equivariant Functions},\nauthor={Edward Berman and Jacob Ginesin and Marco Pacini and Robin Walters},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=rxLUTPLBT3},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"On Uncertainty Calibration for Equivariant Functions"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/9fa053d0a1e0d9e9e7f4d3e787b3aae8af5a9416.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"berman|on_uncertainty_calibration_for_equivariant_functions"},"authorids":{"value":["~Edward_Berman1","~Jacob_Ginesin1","~Marco_Pacini1","~Robin_Walters1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Yi_Liu12"},"authors":{"value":["Edward Berman","Jacob Ginesin","Marco Pacini","Robin Walters"]}},"tmdate":1769619032173,"pdate":1769619032060,"tcdate":1761573285780,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper6320/Authors"],"forum":"rxLUTPLBT3","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":6320,"cdate":1761573285780,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper6320/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper6320/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1769619032173,"odate":1761766980105,"domain":"TMLR","id":"rxLUTPLBT3","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Rejected by TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-turn debates have gained attention as language evaluation tasks for subject matter comprehension, critical reasoning and long-form responses. Language Models (LMs) play the role of judges for obtaining subjective ratings as a cheap alternative to human labor. However, similar to humans, LM judges may remain unsure of ratings and rate debate arguments either under or over-confidently. We empirically study judge calibration in multi-turn self debates, wherein a single LM debater debates with itself, and uncover that LM judges are often overconfident in their judgements. Miscalibration occurs as model confidence ratings increase while rated scores may decrease over debate rounds. Judge confidence exceeds score ratings for both frontier as well as open-source models. We further show that while naive finetuning may improve calibration by increasing scores, it does not necessarily lower overconfidence in ratings. Finetuned overconfident judges prefer similar ratings as confidence and rate different arguments indistinguishably. Our empirical analysis leads us to an observation that helps mitigate overconfidence. Since lower confidences and scores form the tail end of the dataset and are most desirable from a judge’s perspective, sampling from this left tail must calibrate for confidence. We thus fit a mixture of Gumbels distribution on expected ratings of debate arguments and then rejection sample from its tail to finetune judge models. Sampling from the mixture of Gumbels, when compared to naive ratings and Supervised Finetuning (SFT), lowers judge confidence and yield well-calibrated juddges while learning an expressive multi-modal distribution over ratings. Debate datasets and code will be released as part of the final version."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nanonymous2026understanding,\ntitle={Understanding Judge Calibration in Multi-Turn Debates},\nauthor={Anonymous},\njournal={Submitted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=DQc5tVVx3D},\nnote={Rejected}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Understanding Judge Calibration in Multi-Turn Debates"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/419bb9e9102b4e07d927bd3cf3da83a350ec03ea.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR/Rejected"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Charles_Xu1"}},"tmdate":1782547340789,"tcdate":1772393778848,"writers":["TMLR","TMLR/Paper7721/Authors"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper7721/Authors"],"forum":"DQc5tVVx3D","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":7721,"cdate":1772393778848,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper7721/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Rejected"],"mdate":1782547340789,"odate":1773749419736,"domain":"TMLR","id":"DQc5tVVx3D","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/biomedia-mira/calibration_under_shifts"},"abstract":{"value":"We conduct an extensive study on the state of calibration under real-world dataset shift for image classification. Our work provides important insights on the choice of post-hoc and in-training calibration techniques, and yields practical guidelines for all practitioners interested in robust calibration under shift. We compare various post-hoc calibration methods, and their interactions with common in-training calibration strategies (e.g., label smoothing), across a wide range of natural shifts, on eight different classification tasks across several imaging domains. We find that: (i) simultaneously applying entropy regularisation and label smoothing yield the best calibrated raw probabilities under dataset shift, (ii) post-hoc calibrators exposed to a small amount of semantic out-of-distribution data (unrelated to the task) are most robust under shift, (iii) recent calibration methods specifically aimed at increasing calibration under shifts do not necessarily offer significant improvements over simpler post-hoc calibration methods, (iv) improving calibration under shifts often comes at the cost of worsening in-distribution calibration. Importantly, these findings hold for randomly initialised classifiers, as well as for those finetuned from foundation models, the latter being consistently better calibrated compared to models trained from scratch. Finally, we conduct an in-depth analysis of ensembling effects, finding that (i) applying calibration prior to ensembling (instead of after) is more effective for calibration under shifts, (ii) for ensembles, OOD exposure deteriorates the ID-shifted calibration trade-off, (iii) ensembling remains one of the most effective methods to improve calibration robustness and, combined with finetuning from foundation models, yields best calibration results overall."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nroschewitz2025where,\ntitle={Where are we with calibration under dataset shift in image classification?},\nauthor={M{\\'e}lanie Roschewitz and Raghav Mehta and Fabio De Sousa Ribeiro and Ben Glocker},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=1NYKXlRU2H},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Where are we with calibration under dataset shift in image classification?"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"Camera-ready version"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/499e52462720d28f164e28bdbb0ce7fdc9d72b7d.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"roschewitz|where_are_we_with_calibration_under_dataset_shift_in_image_classification"},"authorids":{"value":["~Mélanie_Roschewitz1","~Raghav_Mehta1","~Fabio_De_Sousa_Ribeiro1","~Ben_Glocker1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Lei_Feng1"},"authors":{"value":["Mélanie Roschewitz","Raghav Mehta","Fabio De Sousa Ribeiro","Ben Glocker"]}},"tmdate":1760119743250,"pdate":1760119743185,"tcdate":1752143663303,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper5352/Authors"],"forum":"1NYKXlRU2H","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":5352,"cdate":1752143663303,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper5352/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper5352/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1760119743250,"odate":1753460427148,"domain":"TMLR","id":"1NYKXlRU2H","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Under review for TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Structured pruning is widely used to reduce the computational and memory requirements of deep neural networks (DNNs), enabling efficient deployment in resource-constrained computer vision systems. However, in safety-critical applications, predictive confidence must also be well calibrated, such that confidence accurately reflects the probability of correctness. Existing structured pruning methods primarily optimize predictive performance or feature importance, while largely overlooking uncertainty calibration (UC) as a pruning objective. To address this gap, we propose, to the best of our knowledge, the first calibration-aware sample selection strategy for relevance-based structured pruning. Instead of estimating filter importance over the entire validation set, the proposed framework identifies calibration-critical spillover samples from reliability diagrams and performs Layer-wise Relevance Propagation  only on this informative subset, enabling pruning decisions that explicitly account for predictive reliability. We evaluate the proposed approach on convolutional neural networks (VGG-16, ResNet-34/56, and DenseNet-121) and a vision transformer (DeiT-Tiny) for the CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and ImageNet datasets. Results show that calibration-aware sample selection can improve ECE and NLL while maintaining competitive predictive accuracy in several pruning regimes, although the gains depend on architecture, sparsity level, and evaluation metric. In the best-performing setting, our method reduces expected calibration error (ECE) by approximately 54\\% relative to conventional structured pruning. Furthermore, the resulting pruned models remain amenable to post-hoc recalibration, reducing calibration error below 2\\% ECE in several pruning settings while preserving predictive performance. These findings demonstrate that calibration-aware sample selection can serve as an effective design principle for structured pruning and highlight the importance of the data used to estimate structural relevance."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nanonymous2026calibrationaware,\ntitle={Calibration-Aware Relevance Estimation for Structured Filter Pruning},\nauthor={Anonymous},\njournal={Submitted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=brVxLE6Kal},\nnote={Under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration-Aware Relevance Estimation for Structured Filter Pruning"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"We substantially revised the manuscript in response to the reviewers. The main changes are:\n\n1. We corrected and clarified the data-split protocol. Calibration analysis and spillover-sample selection now use only a held-out validation set, while the test set remains strictly held out for final evaluation. The affected experiments were rerun under this corrected protocol.\n\n2. We revised Fig. 1, Algorithm 1, and the Method/Experimental Setup sections to make the roles of $D_{\\mathrm{train}}$, $D_{\\mathrm{val}}$, $D_{\\mathrm{spill}}$, and $D_{\\mathrm{test}}$ explicit.\n\n3. We separated the auxiliary confidence-adjustment analysis from the pruning pipeline. The parameters $\\delta_{\\mathrm{over}}$ and $\\delta_{\\mathrm{under}}$ are now diagnostic only and do not affect spillover construction, pruning, fine-tuning, or model selection.\n\n4. We added a controlled sample-selection ablation at 50% pruning, comparing the proposed spillover set with the full validation set and a random matched-size validation subset while keeping the downstream pruning pipeline fixed.\n\n5. We expanded and clarified the experimental analysis, including discussion of architecture-, sparsity-, and metric-dependent behavior, particularly at high pruning levels.\n\n6. We revised the Abstract, Introduction, Results, and Conclusion to moderate claims and clarify that improved calibration does not constitute a formal reliability or safety guarantee.\n\n7. We improved manuscript presentation and consistency, including a redesigned Fig. 1, corrected references/placeholders, harmonized notation, and clearer distinction between the spillover tolerance and the LRP stabilizer."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a8bafb4dca6f90241d36ff63f51043816e770889.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR/Under_Review"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Vasileios_Belagiannis1"}},"tmdate":1791148309338,"tcdate":1784195778295,"writers":["TMLR","TMLR/Paper10582/Authors"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper10582/Authors"],"forum":"brVxLE6Kal","number":10582,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1784195778295,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/Paper10582/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review"],"mdate":1791148309338,"odate":1784547126613,"domain":"TMLR","id":"brVxLE6Kal","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/thomaspzollo/vla_calibration"},"abstract":{"value":"Trustworthy robot behavior requires not only high levels of task success but also that the robot can reliably quantify how likely it is to succeed. To this end, we present a first-of-its-kind study of confidence calibration in vision-language-action (VLA) foundation models, which map visual observations and natural language instructions to low-level robot motor commands. We establish a confidence estimation baseline for VLAs, examine how task success relates to calibration error and how calibration evolves over time, and introduce two lightweight techniques to remedy the miscalibration we observe: prompt ensembles and action-wise Platt scaling. Our aim in this study is to begin to develop the tools and conceptual understanding necessary to render VLAs trustworthy via reliable uncertainty quantification."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nzollo2026confidence,\ntitle={Confidence Calibration in Vision-Language-Action Models},\nauthor={Thomas P Zollo and Richard Zemel},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=OmpTwegbHR},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Confidence Calibration in Vision-Language-Action Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8c5e3c981e98eacd62f5fcf6cb55e5785fd5f860.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"zollo|confidence_calibration_in_visionlanguageaction_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Thomas_P_Zollo1","~Richard_Zemel1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Zhiwen_Fan2"},"authors":{"value":["Thomas P Zollo","Richard Zemel"]}},"tmdate":1793414070445,"pdate":1793414070322,"tcdate":1779098791310,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper9023/Authors"],"forum":"OmpTwegbHR","number":9023,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1779098791310,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper9023/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper9023/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1793414070445,"odate":1780194109495,"domain":"TMLR","id":"OmpTwegbHR","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Rejected by TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"The goal of probabilistic prediction is to issue predictive distributions that are as informative as possible, subject to being calibrated. Despite substantial progress in the univariate setting, achieving multivariate calibration remains challenging. Recent work has introduced pre-rank functions, scalar projections of multivariate forecasts and observations, as flexible diagnostics for assessing specific aspects of multivariate calibration, but their use has largely been limited to post-hoc evaluation. We propose a regularization-based calibration method that enforces multivariate calibration during the training of multivariate distributional regression models using pre-rank functions. We further introduce a novel PCA-based pre-rank that projects predictions onto principal directions of the predictive distribution. Through simulation studies and experiments on 18 real-world multi-output regression datasets, we show that the proposed approach substantially improves multivariate pre-rank calibration without compromising predictive accuracy, and that the PCA pre-rank reveals dependence-structure misspecifications, such as covariance-eigenstructure perturbations, that can go undetected by the existing pre-ranks considered."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nanonymous2026enforcing,\ntitle={Enforcing Multivariate Calibration during Training with Pre-Rank Regularization},\nauthor={Anonymous},\njournal={Submitted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=WtTW6QOvCW},\nnote={Rejected}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Enforcing Multivariate Calibration during Training with Pre-Rank Regularization"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"In response to the reviewers’ feedback, we have added new experimental results in Appendix F, “New Results.”"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/b34d26a2bb319bd4f887fe3273c938774d223d66.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR/Rejected"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Michele_Caprio1"}},"tmdate":1791921201028,"tcdate":1787312690708,"writers":["TMLR","TMLR/Paper11533/Authors"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper11533/Authors"],"forum":"WtTW6QOvCW","number":11533,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1787312690708,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper11533/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Rejected"],"mdate":1791921201028,"odate":1787554609968,"domain":"TMLR","id":"WtTW6QOvCW","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/luferrer/expected_cost"},"abstract":{"value":"Most machine learning classifiers are designed to output posterior probabilities for the classes given the input sample. These probabilities may be used to make the categorical decision on the class of the sample; provided as input to a downstream system; or provided to a human for interpretation. Evaluating the quality of the posteriors generated by these system is an essential problem which was addressed decades ago with the invention of proper scoring rules (PSRs). Unfortunately, much of the recent machine learning literature uses calibration metrics---most commonly, the expected calibration error (ECE)---as a proxy to assess posterior performance. The problem with this approach is that calibration metrics reflect only one aspect of the quality of the posteriors, ignoring the discrimination performance. For this reason, we argue that calibration metrics should play no role in the assessment of posterior quality. Expected PSRs should instead be used for this job, preferably normalized for ease of interpretation. In this work, we first give a brief review of PSRs from a practical perspective, motivating their definition using Bayes decision theory. We discuss why expected PSRs provide a principled measure of the quality of a system's posteriors and why calibration metrics are not the right tool for this job. We argue that calibration metrics, while not useful for performance assessment, may be used as diagnostic tools during system development. With this purpose in mind, we discuss a simple and practical calibration metric, called calibration loss, derived from a decomposition of expected PSRs. We compare this metric with the ECE and with the expected score divergence calibration metric from the PSR literature and argue, using theoretical and empirical evidence, that calibration loss is superior to these two metrics."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nferrer2025evaluating,\ntitle={Evaluating Posterior Probabilities:  Decision Theory, Proper Scoring Rules, and Calibration},\nauthor={Luciana Ferrer and Daniel Ramos},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=qbrE0LR7fF},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Evaluating Posterior Probabilities:  Decision Theory, Proper Scoring Rules, and Calibration"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"Camera ready version with a few minor edits and the addition of a paragraph discussing the various notions of calibration that appear in the literature (see the first paragraph in section 2.4), as requested by one of the reviewers and the editor. \n\nA link to a public github repository with the code (which was submitted as supplementary material in previous versions to preserve anonymity) is now included."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7b43d28be9c0315e040f42823e03a931aaf3e3f9.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"ferrer|evaluating_posterior_probabilities_decision_theory_proper_scoring_rules_and_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Luciana_Ferrer1","~Daniel_Ramos1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Takashi_Ishida1"},"authors":{"value":["Luciana Ferrer","Daniel Ramos"]}},"tmdate":1737238635710,"pdate":1737238635615,"tcdate":1724263310036,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper3224/Authors"],"forum":"qbrE0LR7fF","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":3224,"cdate":1724263310036,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper3224/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper3224/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1737238635710,"odate":1724541134723,"domain":"TMLR","id":"qbrE0LR7fF","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/45a7c2d7dce207eb8487e4125fc9caabf3defe98.zip"},"abstract":{"value":"The reliability of probabilistic classifiers hinges on their calibration---the property that their confidence accurately reflect the true class probabilities.\n  The expected calibration error (ECE) is a standard metric for quantifying the calibration of classifiers.\n  However, its estimation presumes access to ground-truth labels.\n  In positive-unlabeled (PU) learning, only positive and unlabeled data are available, which makes the standard ECE estimator inapplicable.\n  Although PU learning has been extensively studied for risk estimation and classifier training, calibration in this setting has received little attention.\n  In this paper, we present PU-ECE, the first ECE estimator for PU data.\n  We provide non-asymptotic bias bounds and prove convergence rates that match those of the fully supervised ECE with an optimal bin size.\n  Furthermore, we develop an information-theoretic generalization error analysis of PU-ECE by formalizing the conditional mutual information (CMI) for a PU setting.\n  Experiments on synthetic and real-world benchmark datasets validate our theoretical analysis and demonstrate that our PU-based ECE estimator achieves performance comparable to that of the fully-labeled ECE estimator."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nkiryo2026estimating,\ntitle={Estimating Expected Calibration Error for Positive-Unlabeled Learning},\nauthor={Ryuichi Kiryo and Futoshi Futami and Masashi Sugiyama},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=SvoBtLIrPZ},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Estimating Expected Calibration Error for Positive-Unlabeled Learning"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"- Updated formatting and style for the camera-ready version.\n- No substantive changes; all reviewer comments were addressed in previous revisions."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f786e7387dbf69e7c06f9c6ea58dcded23606cd1.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"kiryo|estimating_expected_calibration_error_for_positiveunlabeled_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ryuichi_Kiryo1","~Futoshi_Futami1","~Masashi_Sugiyama1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Bruno_Loureiro1"},"authors":{"value":["Ryuichi Kiryo","Futoshi Futami","Masashi Sugiyama"]}},"tmdate":1768214877560,"pdate":1768214877488,"tcdate":1753347876913,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper5463/Authors"],"forum":"SvoBtLIrPZ","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":5463,"cdate":1753347876913,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/Paper5463/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper5463/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1768214877560,"odate":1753699478143,"domain":"TMLR","id":"SvoBtLIrPZ","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Under review for TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Post-training improves the capabilities of large language models but often degrades their confidence calibration. Recent label-free methods use the corresponding pre-trained language model (PLM) as a calibration reference, yet remain miscalibrated when the PLM and post-trained model (PoLM) disagree. We introduce Disagreement-Informed Calibration (DiCal), an unsupervised framework that calibrates agreement and disagreement samples separately. Our analysis identifies a Prediction Divergence Layer (PDL), after which the two models' predictive trajectories separate sharply on disagreement samples. DiCal views their predictions as a candidate set and decomposes PoLM correctness into candidate coverage and conditional correctness, estimated respectively from PLM probability mass and relative post-PDL support. Their product yields a label-free target for learning a disagreement-specific temperature. We further establish an excess-risk guarantee and introduce Agreement-Informed ECE (AIECE) to expose cross-regime error cancellation. Experiments across multiple LLM families and benchmarks demonstrate superior calibration. On MMLU with Qwen3.5-9B, DiCal reduces ECE from $13.766\\%$ to $0.703\\%$ without changing the predictions."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nanonymous2026dical,\ntitle={DiCal: Disagreement-Informed Confidence Calibration for Post-Trained {LLM}s},\nauthor={Anonymous},\njournal={Submitted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=3VNodZn5bd},\nnote={Under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"DiCal: Disagreement-Informed Confidence Calibration for Post-Trained LLMs"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/0ee50705c56678a31eca0e116c95d9ab9fffb50b.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR/Under_Review"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Liang_Ding3"}},"tmdate":1790478630171,"tcdate":1787216638881,"writers":["TMLR","TMLR/Paper11500/Authors"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper11500/Authors"],"forum":"3VNodZn5bd","number":11500,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1787216638881,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review"],"mdate":1790478630171,"odate":1787886981304,"domain":"TMLR","id":"3VNodZn5bd","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Urban mobility digital twins are revolutionizing how cities manage increasingly complex transportation systems, enabling real-time optimization across multiple stakeholders, services, and dynamic operations. Central to these digital twins is the origin-destination (OD) calibration problem—estimating travel demand patterns that produce realistic traffic simulations matching observed conditions. However, existing calibration methods face critical limitations: they require a prohibitively large number of expensive simulation runs and struggle with high-dimensional city-scale networks. To mitigate these issues, we introduce ControlVAE, a novel physics-informed neural network approach for sample-efficient OD calibration. Our method leverages traffic flow patterns, embedded in an auxiliary differentiable physics model, to directly calibrate an interpretable neural representation of the OD matrix from observed data. Specifically, we develop a conditional variational autoencoder framework with a controllable cross-attention mechanism that incorporates this traffic simulation model via differentiable physics knowledge. Across the evaluated benchmarks, ControlVAE improves calibration quality over the SPSA family and neural baselines while reducing expensive SUMO usage, especially in limited-budget regimes."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\ncao2026physicsaware,\ntitle={Physics-Aware Variational Autoencoder for Urban Travel Demand Calibration},\nauthor={Defu Cao and Sam Griesemer and Zijun Cui and Carolina Osorio and Yan Liu},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=r5oS1XXbT3},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Physics-Aware Variational Autoencoder for Urban Travel Demand Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/19708f7c66b205bf2cfb9d6fd272a9e1e624f1cb.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"cao|physicsaware_variational_autoencoder_for_urban_travel_demand_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Defu_Cao1","~Sam_Griesemer1","~Zijun_Cui1","~Carolina_Osorio1","~Yan_Liu1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Tianbao_Yang1"},"authors":{"value":["Defu Cao","Sam Griesemer","Zijun Cui","Carolina Osorio","Yan Liu"]}},"tmdate":1786289021684,"pdate":1786289021620,"tcdate":1760391417945,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper6196/Authors"],"forum":"r5oS1XXbT3","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":6196,"cdate":1760391417945,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/Paper6196/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper6196/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1786289021684,"odate":1761833204568,"domain":"TMLR","id":"r5oS1XXbT3","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Under review for TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent studies have argued normatively, on synthetic data, that evaluating survival models\nby discrimination alone (i.e., via concordance index) produces model comparisons that are\nsystematically misleading, because the metric ignores calibration and time-dependent accu-\nracy. Whether this matters for real, published, non-clinical models has not been tested. We\nreproduce three published survival-ML models across three structurally distinct domains –\nhard-drive failure prediction, peer-to-peer credit default, and user disengagement on digital\nplatforms – validate our evaluation instrument against the anchor paper’s own synthetic ex-\nperiment, and test five pre-registered hypotheses under a Holm-corrected family-wise error\nrate. Three of five reject (though this count is contingent on one pre-registered thresh-\nold that clears by a narrow margin). A model that reproduces the published literature’s\ndiscrimination almost exactly (C = 0.9595 vs. 0.958 reported) fails a formal calibration\ntest at p < 0.001; a broad feature-ablation search finds no single attribute responsible for\nthis discrimination, indicating the calibration failure is not an artifact of a trivial short-\ncut. A lender’s estimated default risk is biased upward by roughly two percentage points,\ngrowing to nearly four points in the riskiest segment, when loan prepayment is treated as\nnon-informative censoring rather than as a competing risk. A platform’s churn model shows\nprobability estimates that degrade with the prediction horizon even as its global discrim-\nination remains within the pre-registered C-index band. A direct test of whether metric\nchoice inverts which model would be preferred does not reject, though with limited statis-\ntical power given only two to three models per domain; the failure mode we document is\nbetter characterized as misplaced confidence in a chosen model than as choice of the wrong\none. We release a reusable, pre-registered evaluation harness, together with full code and a\nsingle annotated notebook, so that these results can be independently verified and the audit\nextended to further domains and models"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nanonymous2026the,\ntitle={The C-index illusion: discrimination without calibration in published survival models},\nauthor={Anonymous},\njournal={Submitted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=I4ijZQ67XR},\nnote={Under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"The C-index illusion: discrimination without calibration in published survival models"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"We revised the manuscript in response to the reviewers comments and made the main claims more closely match the evidence.\n\nFirst, we removed H_meta as a formal omnibus inference. The five hypotheses are now reported separately with Holm-adjusted p-values. Under the literal H3 rule, H2, H3, and H5 reject; under the proportional H3 sensitivity, only H2 and H5 reject. The resulting $3/5$ and $2/5$ counts are reported only as descriptive summaries, with no global p-value or overall reject/non-reject conclusion.\n\nSecond, we revised the interpretation of H5. Higher raw Brier scores across 12, 24, and 36 months are now interpreted as higher absolute prediction error at later horizons, not as evidence that the model itself degrades. We added a post-hoc comparison with a training-set Kaplan-Meier reference. The Brier Skill Scores were $0.1353$, $0.1322$, and $0.1454$ at 12, 24, and 36 months, respectively. The paired-bootstrap contrast was $BSS(36)-BSS(12)=0.0101$, with 95% CI $[-0.0082, 0.0276]$. These results do not support relative deterioration. The temporal-AUC sensitivity analysis is also non-monotonic and does not support temporal decline.\n\nThird, we revised the calibration claims. The manuscript now states explicitly that D-Calibration is interpreted conditional on non-informative censoring and that the Domain 1 result is an in-sample goodness-of-fit result, not out-of-sample predictive validation. We added fixed-horizon calibration analyses at 12, 24, and 36 months, including predicted risk, observed risk, observed-minus-predicted differences, and ICI. The observed-minus-predicted differences are $0.0117$, $0.0134$, and $0.0214$, respectively. We also moderated the wording throughout the manuscript so that the conclusions do not go beyond these assumptions.\n\nFourth, we clarified the role of H3. H3 is now described as an audit of the cumulative-incidence estimator used under competing risks, not as a direct test of the audited Cox model predictions. The manuscript now states explicitly that the observed difference between the naive estimator and the Aalen-Johansen estimator does not show that the Cox models are defective or miscalibrated. We also retained the preregistration ambiguity transparently: the frozen rule used an absolute count of at least three strata, while the observed Bondora data contain seven rating strata. The literal and proportional interpretations are both reported.\n\nWe also improved the clarity of several figures and made small consistency edits across the abstract, methods, results, discussion, conclusion, and protocol appendix. In particular, we softened calibration wording, clarified that downstream discrepancies may arise from models, data, or evaluation procedures, and restored the literal frozen H3 wording in the appendix while keeping the denominator mismatch disclosure.\n\nOverall, the revised manuscript now presents a narrower conclusion: in the evaluated published models and domains, the global C-index does not capture every decision-relevant dimension examined. Calibration analyses, competing-risk-aware evaluation, and horizon-specific proper scores provide complementary information, subject to their assumptions."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d2e5e81073f2fdb925554d71876f3e979465dea8.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR/Under_Review"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Jiwei_Zhao1"}},"tmdate":1789093408963,"tcdate":1784662591174,"writers":["TMLR","TMLR/Paper10715/Authors"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper10715/Authors"],"forum":"I4ijZQ67XR","number":10715,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1784662591174,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/Paper10715/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review"],"mdate":1789093408963,"odate":1786375179485,"domain":"TMLR","id":"I4ijZQ67XR","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/sandruskyi/SocratesLoss"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep neural networks, despite their high accuracy, often exhibit poor confidence calibration, limiting their reliability in high-stakes applications. Current ad-hoc confidence calibration methods attempt to fix this during training but face a fundamental trade-off: two-phase training methods achieve strong classification performance at the cost of training instability and poorer confidence calibration, while single-loss methods are stable but underperform in classification. This paper addresses and mitigates this stability-performance trade-off. We propose Socrates Loss, a novel, unified loss function that explicitly leverages uncertainty by incorporating an auxiliary unknown class, whose predictions directly influence the loss function and a dynamic uncertainty penalty. This unified objective allows the model to be optimized for both classification and confidence calibration simultaneously, without the instability of complex, scheduled losses. We provide theoretical guarantees that our method regularizes the model to prevent miscalibration and overfitting. Across four benchmark datasets and multiple architectures, our comprehensive experiments demonstrate that Socrates Loss consistently improves training stability while achieving more favorable accuracy-calibration trade-off, often converging faster than existing methods."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\ng{\\'o}mez-g{\\'a}lvez2026socrates,\ntitle={Socrates Loss: Unifying Confidence Calibration and Classification by Leveraging the Unknown},\nauthor={Sandra G{\\'o}mez-G{\\'a}lvez and Tobias Olenyi and Gillian Dobbie and Katerina Taskova},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=DONqw1KhHq},\nnote={}\n}"},"video":{"value":"https://youtu.be/7WuSkC-aWW8?si=9fgq5ZN7euIyGZGU"},"title":{"value":"Socrates Loss: Unifying Confidence Calibration and Classification by Leveraging the Unknown"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"Small rewording in the last paragraph of the Introduction, last paragraph of the Abstract, and in the Conclusion to improve readability."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/48165d4348bc9e1e1acf838e1d93d1720293ccb6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"gómezgálvez|socrates_loss_unifying_confidence_calibration_and_classification_by_leveraging_the_unknown"},"authorids":{"value":["~Sandra_Gómez-Gálvez1","~Tobias_Olenyi1","~Gillian_Dobbie1","~Katerina_Taskova1"]},"previous_TMLR_submission_url":{"value":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=S9DGZaDYJP"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Jose_Dolz1"},"authors":{"value":["Sandra Gómez-Gálvez","Tobias Olenyi","Gillian Dobbie","Katerina Taskova"]}},"tmdate":1775842374138,"pdate":1775842373419,"tcdate":1764706293062,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper6786/Authors"],"forum":"DONqw1KhHq","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":6786,"cdate":1764706293062,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper6786/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper6786/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1775842374138,"odate":1765208285141,"domain":"TMLR","id":"DONqw1KhHq","version":2}],"count":1429}